        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Each compound is an ammonium-type cation with a chlorine-containing anion, so find the charge on the anion and then the oxidation number of Cl in it. NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> and N<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub><sup>+</sup> both carry a 1+ charge, so the anion is 1–.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:6.1(1)"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "In ClO<sub>4</sub><sup>–</sup>: Cl + 4(–2) = –1, so Cl = +7. In ClO<sub>3</sub><sup>–</sup>: Cl + 3(–2) = –1, so Cl = +5. In Cl<sup>–</sup>, Cl = –1.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:6.1(1)"
              ]
            }
          ],
